# Super admin

A third portal beside client and employee. One password, one session role, no user row.

- The password lives in `ADMIN_PASS`. `POST /api/auth/admin` is the only place it is read, and it has its own rate-limit bucket in `server/index.ts`. Never ship a default password or echo it back to the client.
- `/admin` is the gate, `/admin/*` is the console behind `AdminLayout`. Reach the gate from an auth screen with `useAdminShortcut()`: A then D then M, with or without Ctrl/Cmd. The hook stays quiet while focus is in a field, so keep that guard if you touch it.
- The gate is a white auth card like `/` and `/signup`: wordmark, `--shadow-auth-card`, `--radius-sm` controls, and a password field. The form is `admin-sign-in` with an off-screen `super-admin` username and `section-admin` autocomplete, so the browser stores it as its own login and does not offer to update a client email on this origin. Do not show that username field. The title is ink like Sign in. The mark is a white `KeyRound` on a rounded `--admin-badge` square filled with `--color-admin-gold` (`--color-gold-400`). No glow. The screen does not go dark.
- Console data goes through `/api/admin` with `requireAuth` + `requireAdmin`, served by `createAdminConsoleRouter` and read in `useAdminEmployees` / `useAdminClients`. The console never calls `/api/employees`. That router is an interface over two contexts: staff accounts joined with onboarding applications. Keep the joining there, not in a service.
- Two ways through the same join. `GET /admin/employees` is staff first, each with the clients instructed to them. `GET /admin/clients` is clients first, each with the adviser they are filed against — including the ones whose slug matches no staff row, which the employees endpoint cannot reach. A screen about people picks the first; a screen about applications picks the second.
- `GET /api/admin/clients` is paged. Query `page`, `pageSize`, and `status` (`all` / `registered` / `pending` / `unassigned`). The answer is a `ClientPage`: `items`, `total`, `pageCount`, and `totals` for every tab so the counts are of the book, not the page. Filter predicates live in `@domain/staff/model.ts` (`matchesClientFilter`) because `registered` and `adviser` are joined, not columns. Cut the window with `@domain/shared/page.ts`. `GET /api/admin/clients/:id` loads one record so a detail URL is not trapped behind the current page.
- `/admin/employees` is a card grid; `/admin/employees/:slug` is one person behind Account and Clients tabs. `/admin/clients` is a table of rows (portrait, name, adviser, applied, status, documents) under All / Registered / Not registered / Unassigned; a row click or the name link opens `/admin/clients/:id` behind Profile, Adviser and Documents tabs. The list uses `useAdminClients`; the record uses `useAdminClient`. Firm PDFs on that Documents tab open `DocumentPreviewDialog` on click; only the client agreement can be drawn today.
- Client records are read-only in the console except the portrait. Applications arrive from the signup form, so do not add an edit form there; changing who holds a client means changing `instructed_person_slug`, which onboarding owns.
- Stored passwords are a dedicated reveal, not a field on the list or the record payload. `GET /api/admin/clients/:id/password` and `GET /api/admin/employees/:slug/password` return `{ password }` (null when the client has no portal account). Identity and staff read their own column; the router only joins the id to the email. Render through `SecretField`: masked until click, fetch on first reveal, click again to hide. Do not put `password` on `GET /employees` or `GET /clients`.
- One client card still lives in `components/admin/ClientCard.tsx`, used on an employee's Clients tab. The firm-wide list is the table, not a second card. Pass `adviser` when the surrounding page does not already say whose client it is.
- Editable: name, role, sort order, password, portrait. The slug and record id are not — clients are filed against the slug. `parseEmployeeAccountPatch` in `@domain/staff/model.ts` is the one rule set; the form and the route both use it.
- Staff portraits go to `PUT /api/admin/employees/:slug/photo` as the original PNG, JPEG, or WebP bytes, parsed by `express.raw` inside the router so the app-wide JSON limit stays at 1mb. The browser does not crop, resize, or re-encode. The console only replaces a portrait: the camera badge on the avatar is the whole control, with no caption and no remove button. `DELETE .../photo` and the hook's `removePhoto` still clear one and are kept for that.
- Every staff save writes a new `<slug>-<epoch>.<ext>` object because `/api/staff/photos` is cached for a day. The extension is the file's own. Naming and the fallback rule live in `@domain/staff/model.ts`: in `photo_storage_path`, null means "fall back to `<slug>.png`" and an empty string means "cleared on purpose" — do not collapse the two. Bytes go through the staff context's `PhotoStore` port, Supabase Storage or memory, with the original content type.
- Clients have the same portrait field. `photo_storage_path` sits on both `public.clients` (the portal account) and `clients_applicatitons` (so an applicant with no account can still have a picture). The console summary carries `photoUrl` (`""` when there is no file). Bytes live in the private `clients` bucket (`supabase/clients.sql`), served behind `GET /api/admin/clients/photos/:file` and, for the signed-in person, `GET /api/me/photo`. Write through `PUT /api/admin/clients/:id/photo`, which also copies the object name onto the matching `clients` row. An empty `photoUrl` is a missing picture, not a missing field — rows still render initials.
- Admin responses carry filing dates, not document field bags. Keep `toClientSummary` collapsing `DocumentsMap` to `saved_at`.
- A client's KYC pack is in that client's `localStorage`. The console cannot read it; say so in copy rather than faking a status.
